    "categorySlug": "pharmacy-pharma",
    "bodyHtml": "<p>In September 2026, around a thousand women a year in England gained access to a treatment that can extend their lives.</p>\n<p>NICE recommended Enhertu for routine NHS use in HER2-low advanced breast cancer, reversing an earlier rejection after a commercial agreement and changes to how NICE assesses value.</p>\n<p>It&#39;s genuinely good news. Charities called it a momentous day.</p>\n<p>And then, quietly, the harder question arrives: <strong>an approval is a permission, not a delivery.</strong> Someone has to prescribe it, prepare it, administer it, monitor it and support the patient through it.</p>\n<p>That someone is a workforce already under strain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changed-at-nice\">What changed at NICE</h2>\n<p>Two technical changes have unlocked a run of decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>The threshold went up.</strong> In April 2026, NICE&#39;s cost-effectiveness threshold rose to <strong>£25,000–£35,000 per quality-adjusted life year</strong>, the first increase since it was introduced in the early 2000s. The <a href=\"https://www.abpi.org.uk/publications/uk-pharmaceutical-investment-competitiveness-report-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ABPI&#39;s 2026 competitiveness report</a> records that nine additional medicines were approved in the three months that followed, covering conditions including blood disorders, autoimmune disease and cancers.</p>\n<p><strong>The method changed.</strong> A new approach to assessing quality-of-life benefits was published on <strong>27 August 2026</strong>, which allowed NICE to revisit previously rejected treatments, as <a href=\"https://www.nationalhealthexecutive.com/articles/enhertu-approved-routine-nhs-use-major-breakthrough-her2-low-breast-cancer-patients\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">National Health Executive reported</a>.</p>\n<p>Enhertu was one of the first beneficiaries. <a href=\"https://www.nursingtimes.net/cancer/nice-approves-life-extending-treatment-for-advanced-breast-cancer-22-09-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nursing Times reported</a> that the change of heart followed a deal with the manufacturers after protocol changes linked to the UK–US pharmaceutical pricing arrangement.</p>\n<p>Fast-track pilots aimed at getting approved medicines to eligible patients sooner, particularly for rare and life-limiting conditions, began testing from September.</p>\n<p>The direction is clear: <strong>more medicines, approved faster.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"the-capacity-question-nobody-puts-in-the-press-release\">The capacity question nobody puts in the press release</h2>\n<p>Modern cancer medicines are rarely a tablet handed over at a counter. Many are infusions or injections that require:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a prescriber and a clinic slot,</li>\n<li>pharmacy <strong>aseptic preparation</strong> in a regulated facility,</li>\n<li>a nurse and a chair to administer them,</li>\n<li>monitoring, toxicity management and follow-up.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each of those is a workforce input. And the evidence says each is tight.</p>\n<p>A <a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42496203/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2026 national evaluation</a> of how UK services have implemented national guidance on managing aseptic capacity pressures in systemic anti-cancer therapy found implementation was selective and variable, with <strong>workforce shortages, infrastructure limits and governance complexity</strong> among the key barriers. Its conclusion was blunt: national guidance alone is not enough without coordinated implementation support and system-level workforce investment.</p>\n<p>On the nursing side, a <a href=\"https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cdp-2025-0186/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">House of Commons Library briefing</a> cited analysis suggesting that a third of acute trusts in England were missing at least 10% of their planned nurses across haematology and oncology wards, based on monthly data from January 2023 to November 2024. The same briefing noted a Royal College of Radiologists survey in which 56% of clinical directors did not believe there were enough radiologists to deliver safe and effective care.</p>\n<p>So the pipeline looks like this: more approvals at the front, constrained capacity in the middle, patients waiting at the end.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-this-matters-beyond-cancer\">Why this matters beyond cancer</h2>\n<p>Cancer is the clearest example, but the pattern is general.</p>\n<p>Every new treatment that reaches NHS patients creates downstream work: clinics, monitoring, pharmacy preparation, diagnostics to identify eligible patients, and specialist nursing to support them.</p>\n<p>Advances in genomics and targeted therapy make this sharper, because eligibility often depends on testing first. More precise medicine means more diagnostic and laboratory work, not less.</p>\n<p>Access policy and workforce policy are the same policy. They just get announced separately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-this-means-for-careers\">What this means for careers</h2>\n<p>If you&#39;re deciding where to specialise, follow the demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Cancer and specialist nursing.</strong> SACT delivery, acute oncology and clinical nurse specialist roles sit at the centre of expanding treatment pathways.</p>\n<p><strong>Pharmacy, especially aseptic and cancer services.</strong> Aseptic preparation is safety-critical, regulated and short-staffed. Technicians, aseptic services staff and cancer pharmacists are in demand, and <a href=\"/blog/pharmacist-independent-prescribers-2026\">prescribing rights now arrive with registration</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Diagnostics and imaging.</strong> Radiographers, biomedical scientists and pathology staff are the gatekeepers of targeted treatment. No test, no eligibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical research.</strong> More medicines means more trials. The <a href=\"/blog/uk-pharmaceutical-industry-jobs-skills-2026\">UK&#39;s clinical research workforce</a> supports tens of thousands of jobs and needs research nurses, coordinators and data specialists.</p>\n<p>These are not fringe niches. They are where treatment expansion meets staffing reality.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-this-means-for-employers-and-system-leaders\">What this means for employers and system leaders</h2>\n<p><strong>Plan capacity alongside approvals.</strong> When a new treatment is recommended for a cohort you serve, model the pharmacy, nursing and chair time it needs before the first referral arrives, not after the waiting list forms.</p>\n<p><strong>Grow your own specialists.</strong> Aseptic and SACT skills are learned on the job. Rotational posts, apprenticeships and structured training convert general staff into the specialists you cannot recruit off the shelf.</p>\n<p><strong>Protect the enabling roles.</strong> Pharmacy technicians, assistants and administrators are often first in line when budgets tighten, and they are precisely the people who free clinicians to treat.</p>\n<section class=\"bottom-line\"><h2 id=\"the-bottom-line\">The bottom line</h2>\n<p>A medicine approval is a promise that only the workforce can keep.</p>\n<p>The UK has spent 2026 making it easier for new treatments to reach patients: a higher NICE threshold, a new valuation method, fast-track pilots and a pricing arrangement that unlocked stalled decisions.</p>\n<p>The next test is whether cancer services, pharmacy and diagnostics have the people to turn those decisions into treatment.</p>\n<p>For anyone choosing a clinical career right now, that&#39;s not just a policy problem. It&#39;s a signal about where the work will be.</p>\n</section>",
